      "fullAnswer": "### 1. Concept of Biogeographic Zones\nBiogeography is the study of the geographical distribution of biological species, communities, and ecosystems across space and geological time. India features rich ecological diversity divided into **10 distinct Biogeographic Zones** based on climate, topography, and native flora/fauna.\n\n### 2. The 10 Biogeographic Zones of India\n1. **Trans-Himalayas:** High-altitude cold desert regions of Ladakh and Lahaul-Spiti. Home to Snow Leopards, Ladakh Urial, and Wild Yaks.\n2. **Himalayas:** Snow-capped mountain chains extending across Jammu & Kashmir, Himachal Pradesh, Uttarakhand, and Sikkim. Characterized by Alpine meadows, Pine forests, Musk Deer, and Himalayan Brown Bears.\n3. **Desert:** Arid sandy regions of Rajasthan (Thar Desert) and salt marshes of Rann of Kutch. Native fauna includes Great Indian Bustard, Blackbuck, and Indian Wild Ass.\n4. **Semi-Arid:** Transition zone between desert and Western Ghats (Gujarat, Punjab, Rajasthan). Features Thorn forests, Asiatic Lions (Gir National Park), and Leopards.\n5. **Western Ghats:** Biodiversity hotspot mountain range along India's western coast. Dense tropical evergreen rainforests supporting Lion-tailed Macaques, Malabar Giant Squirrels, and Nilgiri Tahr.\n6. **Deccan Peninsula:** Semi-arid central plateau region covering Maharashtra, Karnataka, Telangana, and Odisha. Teak forests inhabited by Tigers, Sloth Bears, and Chital.\n7. **Gangetic Plain:** Fertile alluvial plain drained by River Ganges. High agricultural density with Rhinoceros, Swamp Deer, and Gangetic River Dolphins.\n8. **Coasts:** Extensive 7,500 km coastline featuring Mangrove ecosystems (Sundarbans), coral reefs, Olive Ridley Turtles, and Dugongs.\n9. **North-East India:** Biodiversity hotspot with tropical moist forests supporting One-Horned Rhinoceros, Hoolock Gibbons, and Hornbills.\n10. **Islands:** Tropical archipelagos of Andaman, Nicobar, and Lakshadweep containing coral reefs, Andaman Wild Pigs, and Nicobar Megapodes.\n\n### 3. Ecological Significance\nUnderstanding biogeographic zones is essential for establishing targeted National Parks, Wildlife Sanctuaries, and eco-conservation corridors.",
