      "fullAnswer": "### 1. Concept of a MOD-8 Counter\nA **MOD-8 (Modulus-8) Counter** is a digital counter circuit that cycles through **8 unique binary states** (from $000$ to $111$) before resetting back to $000$.\n\n### 2. Required Number of Flip-Flops\nThe number of flip-flops $N$ required to count $M$ modulus states satisfies:\n$$2^{N-1} < M \\le 2^N$$\nFor $M = 8$, $2^3 = 8$, so exactly **3 Flip-Flops** ($FF_0, FF_1, FF_2$) are required.\n\n### 3. Circuit Design & Truth Table\nUsing 3 JK Flip-Flops in asynchronous ripple configuration:\n- $J_0 = K_0 = 1$, $J_1 = K_1 = 1$, $J_2 = K_2 = 1$.\n- External Clock connected to $FF_0$. $Q_0$ output connected to $FF_1$ clock. $Q_1$ output connected to $FF_2$ clock.\n\n| State # | $Q_2$ | $Q_1$ | $Q_0$ | Decimal Equivalent |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **1** | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| **2** | 0 | 0 | 1 | 1 |\n| **3** | 0 | 1 | 0 | 2 |\n| **4** | 0 | 1 | 1 | 3 |\n| **5** | 1 | 0 | 0 | 4 |\n| **6** | 1 | 0 | 1 | 5 |\n| **7** | 1 | 1 | 0 | 6 |\n| **8** | 1 | 1 | 1 | 7 |\n\n### 4. Applications\n- Divide-by-8 frequency division in digital clocks.\n- Octal counter units in computer processor control logic.",
