      "fullAnswer": "### 1. Concept of Indefinite Integration\nIndefinite integration is the process of finding antiderivatives. Every indefinite integral includes an arbitrary constant of integration $C$.\n\n### 2. Solution to Part (i): $\\int (x^2 + 3x + 2) \\, dx$\n\n#### A. Integration Power Rule\n$$\\int x^n \\, dx = \frac{x^{n+1}}{n+1} + C \\quad (n \ne -1)$$\n\n#### B. Term-by-Term Integration\n$$\\int (x^2 + 3x + 2) \\, dx = \\int x^2 \\, dx + 3 \\int x \\, dx + 2 \\int 1 \\, dx$$\n$$= \frac{x^{2+1}}{2+1} + 3 \\cdot \frac{x^{1+1}}{1+1} + 2x + C$$\n$$= \\mathbf{\frac{x^3}{3} + \frac{3x^2}{2} + 2x + C}$$\n\n### 3. Solution to Part (ii): $\\int (\\sin x + \\cos x) \\, dx$\n\n#### A. Standard Trigonometric Integrals\n- $\\int \\sin x \\, dx = -\\cos x$\n- $\\int \\cos x \\, dx = \\sin x$\n\n#### B. Term-by-Term Integration\n$$\\int (\\sin x + \\cos x) \\, dx = \\int \\sin x \\, dx + \\int \\cos x \\, dx$$\n$$= \\mathbf{-\\cos x + \\sin x + C} \\quad \text{or} \\quad \\mathbf{\\sin x - \\cos x + C}$$\n\n### 4. Summary of Results\n- **Part (i):** $\frac{x^3}{3} + \frac{3x^2}{2} + 2x + C$\n- **Part (ii):** $\\sin x - \\cos x + C$",
