      "fullAnswer": "### 1. The Communication Process Model\nCommunication is a dynamic, multi-step process through which a sender encodes and transmits a message through a medium to a receiver, who decodes the message and provides feedback.\n\n### 2. Seven Core Components of the Communication Loop\n\n1. **Sender (Source):** The initiator who conceptualizes the thought, idea, or information to be communicated.\n2. **Encoding:** The process of converting abstract thoughts into transmittable symbols, words, gestures, or graphics.\n3. **Message:** The physical core content or written/spoken information produced after encoding.\n4. **Channel (Medium):** The vehicle or pathway through which the message travels (e.g., email, phone call, face-to-face meeting).\n5. **Receiver:** The target individual or audience who receives the transmitted message signal.\n6. **Decoding:** The mental process by which the receiver interprets and translates the received symbols back into meaningful concepts.\n7. **Feedback:** The receiver's response sent back to the sender, confirming whether the message was accurately understood.\n\n### 3. Role of Noise in Communication\n**Noise** refers to any unwanted disturbance (physical background noise, semantic technical jargon, psychological distraction) that distorts the message during transmission.\n\n### 4. Conclusion\nFeedback completes the communication loop, transforming one-way transmission into effective two-way dialogue.",
