      "fullAnswer": "### 1. Introduction to Air Pollution\nAir pollution is the presence of toxic chemicals, particulate matter ($PM_{2.5}, PM_{10}$), and harmful gases in the atmosphere at concentrations hazardous to human health, livestock, and natural ecosystems.\n\n### 2. Major Air Pollutants & Causes\n1. **Particulate Matter ($PM_{2.5}$ & $PM_{10}$):** Dust, smoke, and soot emitted by thermal power plants, construction sites, and vehicle exhausts.\n2. **Sulfur Dioxide ($SO_2$) & Nitrogen Oxides ($NO_x$):** Emitted from burning coal and diesel, forming atmospheric acid rain.\n3. **Carbon Monoxide ($CO$):** Incomplete fuel combustion in automobiles impairing oxygen transport in human blood.\n4. **Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs):** Refrigerants causing stratospheric ozone layer depletion.\n\n### 3. Health & Environmental Impacts\n- Respiratory diseases (asthma, bronchitis, lung cancer).\n- Acid rain damaging forests, aquatic life, and marble monuments (Taj Mahal corrosion).\n- Global warming and ozone hole formation.\n\n### 4. The Air (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1981\n- Established Central and State Pollution Control Boards (CPCB/SPCB) to set industrial ambient air quality standards.\n- Empowers SPCBs to inspect factories, restrict toxic stack emissions, and enforce non-compliance penalties (expanded to include Noise Pollution in 1987).",
