      "fullAnswer": "### 1. Definition\nEcological succession is the sequential, predictable replacement of one plant and animal community by another over time until a stable **Climax Community** is established.\n\n### 2. Hydrosere (Aquatic Succession)\nStarts in fresh water (pond/lake) and develops into a terrestrial forest climax:\n1. **Phytoplankton Stage (Pioneer)** (*Diatoms, Green Algae*)\n2. **Submerged Plant Stage** (*Hydrilla, Vallisneria*)\n3. **Floating Plant Stage** (*Lotus, Nymphaea*)\n4. **Reed-Swamp Stage** (*Typha, Phragmites*)\n5. **Sedge-Meadow Stage** (*Carex, Juncus*)\n6. **Woodland Stage** (*Salix, Populus*)\n7. **Climax Forest Stage** (*Oak, Teak Forest*)\n\n### 3. Xerosere (Dry Rock Succession)\nStarts on dry bare rock:\n1. **Crustose Lichen Stage (Pioneer)** (*Rhizocarpon* weathering rock into soil)\n2. **Foliose Lichen Stage** (*Parmelia*)\n3. **Moss Stage** (*Polytrichum, Funaria*)\n4. **Herbaceous Stage** (*Annual grasses*)\n5. **Shrub Stage** (*Rhus, Zizyphus*)\n6. **Climax Forest Stage** (*Mature Forest*)\n\n### 4. Conclusion\nSuccession transforms extreme pond or bare rock environments into fertile climax forests.",
