      "fullAnswer": "### 1. Definition of Soil Pollution\nSoil pollution refers to the build-up of toxic chemical compounds, radioactive materials, heavy metals, and disease-causing pathogens in topsoil layers, degrading agricultural fertility and environmental health.\n\n### 2. Major Sources of Soil Contamination\n1. **Industrial Chemical Wastes:** Factories discharge untreated toxic heavy metals (mercury, lead, cadmium, chromium), petroleum hydrocarbons, and acidic sludge directly onto soil surfaces.\n2. **Agricultural Agrochemicals:** Overuse of synthetic chemical fertilizers (nitrates, phosphates), toxic pesticides (DDT, endosulfan), and herbicides contaminates soil microorganisms and accumulates in food crops.\n3. **Municipal Solid Waste & E-Waste:** Non-biodegradable plastic packaging, electronic e-waste, battery heavy metals, and unmanaged municipal landfills leak toxic leachate into subsoil layers.\n4. **Acid Rain & Mining Tailings:** Atmospheric sulfur/nitrogen acid rain and toxic metal tailings from mining operations alter soil pH, leaching essential mineral nutrients.\n\n### 3. Prevention & Remediation\nImplementing organic farming, proper hazardous industrial waste treatment, plastic recycling, and **bioremediation** using soil microbes restores contaminated land.",
