      "fullAnswer": "### 1. Introduction\nPopulation growth refers to the net change in the total number of individuals of a biological species residing within a specified geographical boundary over time.\n\n### 2. Primary Biological Factors\n1. **Birth Rate (Natality):** Total number of live births per 1,000 individuals per year. High natality expands overall population density.\n2. **Death Rate (Mortality):** Total number of deaths per 1,000 individuals per year. Improvements in medical sanitation and healthcare reduce mortality rates.\n3. **Migration (Immigration & Emigration):**\n   - *Immigration:* Inward movement of individuals into a region (++ population).\n   - *Emigration:* Outward movement of individuals out of a region (-- population).\n\n### 3. Environmental & Socio-Economic Drivers\n- **Resource Availability (Carrying Capacity):** Access to clean drinking water, arable agricultural land, food supplies, and shelter.\n- **Healthcare & Family Planning:** Access to modern medical vaccines, reproductive health education, and contraception facilities.\n\n### 4. Conclusion\nPopulation growth is governed by the equilibrium between natality, mortality, net migration, and environmental carrying capacity.",
