      "fullAnswer": "### 1. Definition & Waveform Type\n- **Analog Signal:** Continuous electrical waveform representing physical variables (temperature, sound waves, pressure) that vary smoothly over time.\n- **Digital Signal:** Discrete electrical pulses representing data in binary form (logic `0` and logic `1`) switching between distinct voltage levels.\n\n### 2. Key Differences Comparison\n\n| Feature | Analog Signal | Digital Signal |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Representation** | Continuous sine waves | Discrete square binary pulses |\n| **Noise Susceptibility** | High (susceptible to distortion & atmospheric noise) | Low (immune to external noise; easily restored) |\n| **Storage & Processing** | Difficult to store; hard to encrypt | Easy to store in digital memory RAM/SSDs & encrypt |\n| **Bandwidth** | Requires lower bandwidth | Requires higher bandwidth |\n| **Examples** | Human voice, analog clock, mercury thermometer | Digital computer memory, CDs, USB flash drives |\n\n### 3. Conclusion\nDigital signals have largely replaced analog signals in computing due to superior noise immunity, easy storage, and high transmission accuracy.",
