      "fullAnswer": "### 1. Introduction to JSON Format\nJSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a lightweight, text-based data interchange format that supports six fundamental primitive and composite data types.\n\n### 2. Six Basic JSON Data Types\n1. **String:** Double-quoted Unicode text character sequence (e.g., `\"course\": \"BCA\"`).\n2. **Number:** Double-precision integer or floating-point number without quotes (e.g., `\"score\": 95`, `\"pi\": 3.14`).\n3. **Boolean:** Logical literals `true` or `false` in lowercase (e.g., `\"isEnrolled\": true`).\n4. **Object:** Unordered key-value pairs enclosed in `{}` curly braces (e.g., `\"user\": { \"id\": 1, \"name\": \"Nadeer\" }`).\n5. **Array:** Ordered list sequence of JSON values enclosed in `[]` brackets (e.g., `\"marks\": [85, 90, 95]`).\n6. **Null:** Explicit representation of an empty value using literal `null` (e.g., `\"middleName\": null`).\n\n### 3. Invalid JSON Types\nFunctions, undefined, dates, and circular references are invalid in raw JSON syntax.",
