      "fullAnswer": "### 1. Domain and Range Concepts\n- **Domain:** Set of all valid real input values $x$ for which $f(x)$ yields defined real output values.\n- **Range:** Set of all resulting real output values $y = f(x)$ produced across the domain.\n\n### 2. Analysis for $f(x) = x^2$\n- **Domain:** $x$ can take any real number value without division by zero or negative square roots.\n  $$\text{Domain of } f(x) = (-\\infty, +\\infty) \text{ or } \\mathbb{R}$$\n- **Range:** Squaring any real number yields non-negative real numbers ($x^2 \\ge 0$).\n  $$\text{Range of } f(x) = [0, +\\infty)$$\n\n### 3. Analysis for $g(x) = \\sqrt{x}$\n- **Domain:** Under real numbers, square roots of negative numbers are undefined. Hence $x \\ge 0$.\n  $$\text{Domain of } g(x) = [0, +\\infty)$$\n- **Range:** Principal square root outputs are non-negative real numbers ($\\sqrt{x} \\ge 0$).\n  $$\text{Range of } g(x) = [0, +\\infty)$$\n\n### 4. Conclusion\n$f(x)=x^2$ accepts all real inputs yielding $[0, \\infty)$. $g(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ restricts inputs to $[0, \\infty)$ yielding $[0, \\infty)$.",
