    "whyImportant": [
      "Official MQP Section B Question (5 Marks)",
      "Guaranteed Core Syllabus Topic"
    ],
    "modelAnswer": {
      "fullAnswer": "### 1. Fundamental Theorem of Calculus\nThe definite integral of a function $f(x)$ from lower limit $a$ to upper limit $b$ is evaluated using antiderivative $F(x)$:\n$$\\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \\, dx = [F(x)]_{a}^{b} = F(b) - F(a)$$\n\n### 2. Step-by-Step Integration\n\n#### A. Find Antiderivative of $f(x) = 2x$\n$$\\int 2x \\, dx = 2 \\cdot \frac{x^2}{2} = x^2$$\n\n#### B. Evaluate Definite Limits from $x=1$ to $x=3$\n$$\\int_{1}^{3} 2x \\, dx = \\left[ x^2 \right]_{1}^{3}$$\n\n#### C. Substitute Upper and Lower Limits\n$$= (3)^2 - (1)^2 = 9 - 1 = 8$$\n\n### 3. Conclusion\nThe definite integral $\\int_{1}^{3} 2x \\, dx = 8$.",
      "quickMemory": "DEFINITE INTEGRAL (5 MARKS)\nIntegral(2x dx) from 1 to 3\nAntiderivative = x^2\n[x^2]_1^3 = (3)^2 - (1)^2 = 9 - 1 = 8",
      "keywords": [
        "Definite integral",
        "Antiderivative",
        "Fundamental theorem of calculus",
        "Evaluation"
      ],
      "markCoverage": "5-Mark short answer (150\u2013250 words) with definition, key points, and clear conclusion.",
      "whatToWrite": [
        "State antiderivative of 2x is x^2.",
        "Apply limits from x=1 to x=3.",
        "Calculate 9 - 1 = 8."
      ],
      "twoMinRevision": "The definite integral of 2x from x=1 to x=3 equals 8."
    }
