      "fullAnswer": "### 1. Overview of C Language\nC is a procedural, compiled, middle-level programming language developed by Dennis Ritchie at Bell Labs in 1972. It forms the backbone of operating systems (Linux, Windows kernel) and database engines.\n\n### 2. Key Characteristics\n1. **Procedural & Structured Language:** Programs are structured into modular functions, enhancing code reusability and readability.\n2. **Middle-Level Language:** Combines high-level language abstractions with low-level assembly features (direct hardware address access via pointers).\n3. **Portability:** C code written on one system can compile and run on other hardware architectures with minimal modification.\n4. **Rich Library Functions & Speed:** Built-in standard functions (`<stdio.h>`, `<stdlib.h>`) compile directly into machine instructions, delivering high execution speed.\n5. **Dynamic Memory Allocation:** Supports manual memory management at runtime using `malloc()`, `calloc()`, `realloc()`, and `free()`.\n\n### 3. Conclusion\nC remains fundamental in software engineering due to its speed, efficiency, hardware control, and influence on modern languages (C++, Java, C#).",
