      "fullAnswer": "### 1. Purpose of const Keyword\nIn C programming, the `const` type qualifier is used to declare read-only variables whose values cannot be modified by the program after initial definition.\n\n### 2. Syntax & Key Applications\n1. **Constant Variables:**\n   ```c\n   const float PI = 3.14159;\n   // PI = 3.14; // Compilation Error: assignment of read-only variable 'PI'\n   ```\n2. **Constant Pointers:**\n   - `const int *ptr`: Pointer to a constant integer (data cannot be modified via pointer).\n   - `int * const ptr`: Constant pointer to an integer (pointer address cannot change).\n3. **Function Parameters:** Passing pointer parameters as `const` prevents function side-effects from accidentally mutating caller data.\n   ```c\n   void displayString(const char *str);\n   ```\n\n### 3. Significance & Benefits\n- Prevents accidental data corruption.\n- Improves code readability and self-documentation.\n- Enables compiler optimizations in read-only ROM memory.\n\n### 4. Conclusion\nUsing `const` enforces strict data immutability and memory safety.",
