      "fullAnswer": "### 1. Purpose of if-else Decision Control Structure\nThe `if-else` statement is a two-way conditional decision-making control structure that executes one block of code if a relational condition evaluates to true, and an alternate block if false.\n\n### 2. Syntax & Flowchart\n```c\nif (condition) {\n    // Code block executed if condition is non-zero (true)\n} else {\n    // Code block executed if condition is zero (false)\n}\n```\n\n### 3. C Code Example\n```c\n#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int score = 75;\n    if (score >= 50) {\n        printf(\"Result: PASSED\\n\");\n    } else {\n        printf(\"Result: FAILED\\n\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### 4. Nested if-else-if Ladder\nUsed for testing multiple sequential conditions:\n```c\nif (marks >= 90) grade = 'A';\nelse if (marks >= 75) grade = 'B';\nelse grade = 'C';\n```\n\n### 5. Conclusion\n`if-else` branching controls program execution flow based on dynamic runtime data evaluation.",
